[51.411, "o", "  \u001b[36m\u25cf triage_node\u001b[0m\u001b[2m({\"node\": \"k8s-w1\"})\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[51.538, "o", "    \u001b[2m\u2514\u001b[0m \u001b[31mCRITICAL\u001b[0m k8s-w1: \u001b[1mescalate_hardware\u001b[0m (hardware)\r\n"]
[51.538, "o", "    \u001b[2m\u2514\u001b[0m - Ready=True, cordoned, ReadonlyFilesystem=True; taints node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable:NoSchedule; kubelet v1.36.5\r\n"]
[51.538, "o", "    \u001b[2m\u2514\u001b[0m - cordon reason \"triage: ReadonlyFilesystem: EXT4-fs (vda1) remounted read-only; suspected disk fault, pending hardware check\" (annotation slurm-k8s-lab/triage-reason)\r\n"]
[51.538, "o", "    \u001b[2m\u2514\u001b[0m - ReadonlyFilesystem=True (FilesystemIsReadOnly) since 48s ago: EXT4-fs (vda1): Remounting filesystem read-only\r\n"]
[51.538, "o", "    \u001b[2m\u2514\u001b[0m - 11 workload pod(s) still on the node: argocd/argocd-application-controller-0, argocd/argocd-server-85679c9c4b-4pvtb, cert-manager/cert-manager-cainjector-6468bc96c7-mqkvp, kube-system/coredns-589f44dc88-vzd84, kube-system/coredns-589f44dc88-wbkkg, and 6 more\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  I haven't uncordoned k8s-w1. The disk fault is still active, and nothing shows it has been checked or\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  repaired.\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  \r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  I re-ran triage just now and it still says **critical, `escalate_hardware`**:\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  - `ReadonlyFilesystem=True (FilesystemIsReadOnly) since 48s ago: EXT4-fs (vda1): Remounting filesystem\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  read-only`. The kernel reported this again after the cordon, so it hasn't cleared.\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  - The 11 workload pods, including both CoreDNS replicas, are still on the node.\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  \r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  If I uncordoned it, new pods would land on a read-only root disk and fail. The same thing happened after\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  the uncordon at 21:49: the fault came back at 22:02. The resume tool also refuses a node flagged with a\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  hardware fault unless I pass `force=true`, which is meant for after a repair and burn-in. I won't use it\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  without knowing that has happened.\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  \r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  **What's needed first (from triage):**\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  1. Check the kernel log for I/O errors and the disk's SMART data:\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "     ```\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "     ssh k8s-w1 \"dmesg -T | grep -iE 'I/O error|remount|EXT4-fs error'\"\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "     ```\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  2. If the disk is failing, open a hardware ticket and replace it. If it isn't, run fsck and reboot. A\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  reboot also clears the `ReadonlyFilesystem` condition.\r\n"]
[58.144, "o", "  3. Once `ReadonlyFilesystem=False`, I'll re-run triage and resume the node without `force`.\r\n"]
[58.145, "o", "  \r\n"]
[58.145, "o", "  If the disk has already been checked or fixed, tell me what was done. For example: SMART clean, fsck run,\r\n"]
[58.145, "o", "  node rebooted. If the repair is confirmed and you still want it back in service even though the check\r\n"]
[58.145, "o", "  hasn't cleared yet, say you want a **forced** resume and I'll pass `force=true`.\r\n"]
